      "question": "A team has coding standards that should apply whenever a developer edits a Python file anywhere in the project \u2014 whether it is in `src/`, `tests/`, `scripts/`, or any other directory. A developer proposes creating a directory-level CLAUDE.md in each directory that contains Python files.\n\nWhat is a better approach than placing directory-level CLAUDE.md files in every directory?\n\n- **A.** Put all rules in the project-level CLAUDE.md with conditional instructions like \"If editing a Python file, follow these rules\"\n- **B.** Use `.claude/rules/` with YAML frontmatter specifying glob patterns like `**/*.py` \u2014 the rules auto-load when editing any matching Python file, regardless of directory\n- **C.** Create a single directory-level CLAUDE.md in the `src/` directory and instruct developers to only put Python files there\n- **D.** Use `@import` in the project CLAUDE.md to include a Python rules file that is always loaded",
      "correctAnswer": "Use `.claude/rules/` with YAML frontmatter specifying glob patterns like `**/*.py` \u2014 the rules auto-load when editing any matching Python file, regardless of directory.\n\n`.claude/rules/` files with YAML frontmatter and glob patterns are designed for exactly this use case \u2014 applying rules based on file patterns across the entire project. A rule with a `**/*.py` glob pattern auto-loads whenever any Python file is being edited, regardless of which directory it is in. This is better than directory-level CLAUDE.md for cross-directory concerns. Option A relies on the model correctly interpreting conditional instructions, which is less reliable than automatic pattern-based loading. Option C artificially restricts project structure and does not cover existing Python files in other directories. Option D always loads the rules even when the developer is not editing Python files, adding unnecessary context.",
      "explanation": "Use `.claude/rules/` with YAML frontmatter specifying glob patterns like `**/*.py` \u2014 the rules auto-load when editing any matching Python file, regardless of directory.\n\n`.claude/rules/` files with YAML frontmatter and glob patterns are designed for exactly this use case \u2014 applying rules based on file patterns across the entire project. A rule with a `**/*.py` glob pattern auto-loads whenever any Python file is being edited, regardless of which directory it is in. This is better than directory-level CLAUDE.md for cross-directory concerns. Option A relies on the model correctly interpreting conditional instructions, which is less reliable than automatic pattern-based loading. Option C artificially restricts project structure and does not cover existing Python files in other directories. Option D always loads the rules even when the developer is not editing Python files, adding unnecessary context."
