      "question": "A senior developer needs to redesign the authentication module of their application. The change involves modifying interfaces across multiple files, updating the database schema, and ensuring backward compatibility. They start by asking Claude Code to immediately implement the changes.\n\nWhat workflow approach would be more appropriate for this type of task?\n\n- **A.** Direct implementation is fine for experienced developers \u2014 plan mode is only for juniors\n- **B.** Use plan mode first to have Claude analyse the codebase, propose an architecture, and identify affected files before any implementation begins \u2014 complex architectural changes benefit from planning before execution\n- **C.** Use the explore subagent to automatically implement changes across all files simultaneously\n- **D.** Start implementation immediately but use `--resume` to undo changes if they are wrong",
      "correctAnswer": "Use plan mode first to have Claude analyse the codebase, propose an architecture, and identify affected files before any implementation begins \u2014 complex architectural changes benefit from planning before execution.\n\nPlan mode is appropriate for complex and architectural tasks where understanding the full scope and dependencies before implementation reduces risk. This task involves cross-file interface changes, schema modifications, and backward compatibility \u2014 all of which benefit from a plan-then-execute approach. Option A is wrong because plan mode is about task complexity, not developer experience. Option C misuses the explore subagent \u2014 it is for investigation and understanding, not for implementation. Option D is wrong because `--resume` continues a session; it does not undo changes, and implementing without planning for an architectural change risks cascading errors.",
      "explanation": "Use plan mode first to have Claude analyse the codebase, propose an architecture, and identify affected files before any implementation begins \u2014 complex architectural changes benefit from planning before execution.\n\nPlan mode is appropriate for complex and architectural tasks where understanding the full scope and dependencies before implementation reduces risk. This task involves cross-file interface changes, schema modifications, and backward compatibility \u2014 all of which benefit from a plan-then-execute approach. Option A is wrong because plan mode is about task complexity, not developer experience. Option C misuses the explore subagent \u2014 it is for investigation and understanding, not for implementation. Option D is wrong because `--resume` continues a session; it does not undo changes, and implementing without planning for an architectural change risks cascading errors."
