      "question": "A developer is working with Claude Code to implement a REST API. They have written a detailed specification but notice that Claude keeps generating endpoints with inconsistent naming conventions \u2014 sometimes using camelCase, sometimes snake_case, and sometimes kebab-case. They have tried asking Claude to \"be consistent\" but the problem persists.\n\nWhat is the most effective way to resolve the naming inconsistency?\n\n- **A.** Add a rule to CLAUDE.md stating \"Use consistent naming conventions\"\n- **B.** Provide concrete examples as the first line of defence: \"All endpoint paths must use kebab-case (e.g., `/user-profiles`, `/order-items`). All JSON fields must use camelCase (e.g., `firstName`, `orderDate`).\"\n- **C.** Switch to a different model that is more consistent with naming\n- **D.** Manually fix each inconsistency after Claude generates the code",
      "correctAnswer": "Provide concrete examples as the first line of defence: \"All endpoint paths must use kebab-case (e.g., `/user-profiles`, `/order-items`). All JSON fields must use camelCase (e.g., `firstName`, `orderDate`).\"\n\nConcrete examples are the first-line fix for inconsistency problems. Showing the model exactly what the expected format looks like with specific examples eliminates ambiguity. The model can follow a clear, concrete pattern far more reliably than a vague instruction. Option A is too vague \u2014 \"be consistent\" does not specify which convention to use and is effectively what the developer already tried. Option C is unnecessary when the issue is instruction clarity, not model capability. Option D is a reactive workaround that wastes time on every generation rather than solving the root cause.",
