      "correctAnswer": "Use test-driven iteration \u2014 write a few initial tests capturing core behaviour, have Claude implement them, then write more tests based on what is learned, iterating toward the final design.\n\nTest-driven iteration is ideal for design discovery. Writing a few tests first captures the core expected behaviour without over-committing to a design. Claude implements those tests, the developer evaluates, and then writes additional tests that refine or extend the design. This is iterative by nature. Option A contradicts the goal \u2014 the developer explicitly does not know the full design upfront. Option C undermines test-driven development because the model may generate tests that match its own implementation rather than capturing independent design intent. Option D is better suited for unfamiliar domains where the developer lacks domain knowledge; here the developer understands the domain but wants to iterate on the API design.",
