      "question": "A team has Claude Code running in their CI pipeline for automated code review. They want the output in a structured format that conforms to a specific schema so their downstream tools can parse the results. They also need to ensure the review is thorough, so a developer suggests having Claude review the code in the same session that generated it.\n\nWhat is the correct approach for structured CI output and effective review?\n\n- **A.** Parse Claude's natural language output with regex to extract structured data, and have Claude review code in the same session for continuity\n- **B.** Use `--output-format json` combined with `--json-schema` to get structured output conforming to the required schema, and use a separate session for review because the same session is less effective at self-review\n- **C.** Use the Batch API for pre-merge reviews to save costs, and format output manually in a post-processing step\n- **D.** Set `--output-format json` only, without a schema, and have Claude review in the same session to leverage existing context",
