      "question": "A compliance team deploys an AI-powered document classifier that flags customer communications across five risk categories. After launch, the \"potential threat\" category produces a 40% false positive rate while the other four categories perform well. The team notices that reviewers have begun ignoring flags across all categories, including legitimate ones.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate immediate action to restore reviewer trust in the system?\n\n- **A.** Add a confidence score to each flag so reviewers can prioritize high-confidence results across all categories\n- **B.** Temporarily disable the \"potential threat\" category while improving its prompt criteria, and keep the other four categories active\n- **C.** Write more detailed prose instructions explaining when documents should be classified as \"potential threat\"\n- **D.** Retrain reviewers on the importance of treating each category independently",
      "correctAnswer": "Temporarily disable the \"potential threat\" category while improving its prompt criteria, and keep the other four categories active.\n\nHigh false positive rates in one category destroy trust in ALL categories \u2014 reviewers lose confidence in the entire system, not just the problematic category. Temporarily disabling the high-FP category while improving its prompts is the correct remediation. Option A fails because LLM self-confidence is poorly calibrated, so confidence scores will not reliably separate true from false positives. Option C (more prose instructions) is the kind of vague guidance that tends to underperform compared to concrete examples. Option D addresses symptoms rather than the root cause \u2014 the system itself is generating untrustworthy results.",
