      "correctAnswer": "Include concrete code examples for each severity level showing why a specific vulnerability maps to that rating.\n\nSeverity calibration requires concrete code examples, not prose descriptions. Showing the model actual vulnerability code and explaining why each maps to a specific severity level anchors its judgments consistently. Option A (\"be conservative\") is exactly the kind of vague instruction that fails \u2014 it provides no concrete boundary between severity levels. Option C (temperature=0) does not fix the underlying inconsistency in how the model interprets severity criteria; it only makes the model deterministic for identical inputs. Option D fails because LLM self-confidence is poorly calibrated, so confidence-based filtering will not reliably improve accuracy.",
      "explanation": "Include concrete code examples for each severity level showing why a specific vulnerability maps to that rating.\n\nSeverity calibration requires concrete code examples, not prose descriptions. Showing the model actual vulnerability code and explaining why each maps to a specific severity level anchors its judgments consistently. Option A (\"be conservative\") is exactly the kind of vague instruction that fails \u2014 it provides no concrete boundary between severity levels. Option C (temperature=0) does not fix the underlying inconsistency in how the model interprets severity criteria; it only makes the model deterministic for identical inputs. Option D fails because LLM self-confidence is poorly calibrated, so confidence-based filtering will not reliably improve accuracy."
