      "question": "A data extraction pipeline processes invoices from hundreds of vendors, each with different layouts. The model correctly extracts most fields but occasionally fabricates values for fields like \"purchase order number\" when the invoice does not contain one, and sometimes returns inconsistent JSON formatting.\n\nWhich combination of techniques best addresses BOTH the fabrication and formatting issues?\n\n- **A.** Add few-shot examples with reasoning and increase the temperature to encourage creative extraction\n- **B.** Use tool_use with a JSON schema that includes nullable fields for optional data like purchase order numbers\n- **C.** Add more detailed text instructions specifying every possible invoice layout the model may encounter\n- **D.** Implement confidence-based filtering to discard extractions where the model reports low certainty",
      "correctAnswer": "Use tool_use with a JSON schema that includes nullable fields for optional data like purchase order numbers.\n\ntool_use with JSON schemas eliminates syntax/formatting errors (the most reliable method for structured output), while nullable/optional fields prevent the model from fabricating values for missing information \u2014 the model can return null instead of inventing a purchase order number. Option A addresses formatting inconsistency (few-shot helps) but does not address fabrication; increasing temperature would make both problems worse. Option C cannot enumerate every layout and does not structurally prevent fabrication. Option D fails because LLM self-confidence is poorly calibrated and cannot reliably identify fabricated values.",
