      "question": "A legal tech company uses Claude to classify contract clauses. The model sometimes selects a specific clause type when the language is genuinely ambiguous. The team wants the model to signal ambiguity rather than force a classification.\n\nWhat schema design pattern best handles genuinely ambiguous cases?\n\n- **A.** Add a confidence score field and filter out results below a threshold\n- **B.** Use an enum that includes \"unclear\" and an \"other\" option paired with a detail field for the model to explain its reasoning\n- **C.** Instruct the model in prose to \"use its best judgment when clauses are ambiguous\"\n- **D.** Run the classification twice and only accept results where both runs agree",
      "correctAnswer": "Use an enum that includes \"unclear\" and an \"other\" option paired with a detail field for the model to explain its reasoning.\n\nThe enum with \"unclear\" and \"other\" plus a detail field is the standard pattern for handling ambiguity in structured output. It gives the model a legitimate way to express uncertainty without forcing a potentially incorrect classification. Option A fails because LLM self-confidence is poorly calibrated \u2014 confidence scores do not reliably reflect actual ambiguity. Option C provides vague instructions that do not give the model a structured way to express uncertainty. Option D (running twice) adds cost and latency but does not give the model a mechanism to communicate ambiguity in either run.",
      "explanation": "Use an enum that includes \"unclear\" and an \"other\" option paired with a detail field for the model to explain its reasoning.\n\nThe enum with \"unclear\" and \"other\" plus a detail field is the standard pattern for handling ambiguity in structured output. It gives the model a legitimate way to express uncertainty without forcing a potentially incorrect classification. Option A fails because LLM self-confidence is poorly calibrated \u2014 confidence scores do not reliably reflect actual ambiguity. Option C provides vague instructions that do not give the model a structured way to express uncertainty. Option D (running twice) adds cost and latency but does not give the model a mechanism to communicate ambiguity in either run."
