      "question": "A company needs to process 50,000 customer support tickets to generate classification tags and sentiment scores. The results feed into a monthly analytics dashboard due in 48 hours. No real-time user is waiting for individual results.\n\nWhich processing approach is most cost-effective and appropriate for this workload?\n\n- **A.** Process all tickets synchronously using parallel API calls to complete within a few hours\n- **B.** Use the Message Batches API, which offers 50% cost savings with results delivered within up to 24 hours and no latency SLA\n- **C.** Use the Message Batches API with multi-turn tool calling to handle complex tickets that require follow-up queries\n- **D.** Split into 10 synchronous batches and process sequentially to avoid rate limits",
      "correctAnswer": "Use the Message Batches API, which offers 50% cost savings with results delivered within up to 24 hours and no latency SLA.\n\nThe Message Batches API is designed for latency-tolerant workloads (like overnight reports and periodic analytics), offering 50% cost savings with results in up to 24 hours. With a 48-hour deadline, there is a 24-hour buffer even in the worst case. Option A works functionally but costs twice as much with no benefit since no one is waiting for real-time results. Option C is incorrect because the Batches API does not support multi-turn tool calling. Option D is an unnecessary workaround that does not achieve the cost savings of the Batches API.",
