      "question": "A quality assurance team asks Claude to review its own AI-generated code analysis from earlier in the same conversation. The self-review consistently confirms the original findings without identifying errors that human reviewers later catch.\n\nWhat is the most likely reason the self-review is ineffective, and what is the best alternative?\n\n- **A.** The model needs extended thinking enabled to perform deeper self-review within the same session\n- **B.** The same session retains reasoning context, so the model tends to confirm its prior conclusions; an independent instance without prior context catches more errors\n- **C.** Self-review fails because the model's context window is too small to hold both the original analysis and the review\n- **D.** The model needs a higher temperature setting during self-review to explore alternative interpretations",
      "correctAnswer": "The same session retains reasoning context, so the model tends to confirm its prior conclusions; an independent instance without prior context catches more errors.\n\nSelf-review within the same session is limited because the model retains its original reasoning context and tends to confirm prior conclusions. An independent instance \u2014 a separate API call with no shared conversation history \u2014 is not anchored to the original reasoning and catches more errors. Option A (extended thinking) does not overcome the fundamental bias of reviewing one's own work in the same context. Option C (context window size) is not the issue; larger context windows do NOT fix attention dilution. Option D (higher temperature) adds randomness but does not remove the anchoring to prior reasoning.",
      "explanation": "The same session retains reasoning context, so the model tends to confirm its prior conclusions; an independent instance without prior context catches more errors.\n\nSelf-review within the same session is limited because the model retains its original reasoning context and tends to confirm prior conclusions. An independent instance \u2014 a separate API call with no shared conversation history \u2014 is not anchored to the original reasoning and catches more errors. Option A (extended thinking) does not overcome the fundamental bias of reviewing one's own work in the same context. Option C (context window size) is not the issue; larger context windows do NOT fix attention dilution. Option D (higher temperature) adds randomness but does not remove the anchoring to prior reasoning."
