      "question": "A research team processes a large codebase through Claude, performing a security audit. Individual file analyses are thorough, but the final report misses vulnerabilities that span multiple files, such as an input validated in one file but used unsanitized in another.\n\nWhat multi-pass strategy best addresses cross-file vulnerability detection?\n\n- **A.** Increase the context window and submit all files in a single prompt so the model can see everything at once\n- **B.** Use a per-file local analysis pass followed by a cross-file integration pass that synthesizes findings across file boundaries\n- **C.** Run the same single-pass analysis multiple times and merge the results to catch missed vulnerabilities\n- **D.** Add a prompt instruction telling the model to \"pay special attention to cross-file dependencies\"",
      "correctAnswer": "Use a per-file local analysis pass followed by a cross-file integration pass that synthesizes findings across file boundaries.\n\nThe multi-pass approach of per-file local analysis followed by a cross-file integration pass is designed exactly for this scenario. The local pass captures file-level details, and the integration pass connects findings across file boundaries to detect vulnerabilities like unsanitized inputs flowing between files. Option A fails because larger context windows do NOT fix attention dilution \u2014 cramming more files into one prompt makes it harder, not easier, to track cross-file relationships. Option C (repeated single-pass) will likely miss the same cross-file issues each time since the approach itself is the problem. Option D is a vague instruction that does not provide the structural mechanism needed for cross-file analysis.",
      "explanation": "Use a per-file local analysis pass followed by a cross-file integration pass that synthesizes findings across file boundaries.\n\nThe multi-pass approach of per-file local analysis followed by a cross-file integration pass is designed exactly for this scenario. The local pass captures file-level details, and the integration pass connects findings across file boundaries to detect vulnerabilities like unsanitized inputs flowing between files. Option A fails because larger context windows do NOT fix attention dilution \u2014 cramming more files into one prompt makes it harder, not easier, to track cross-file relationships. Option C (repeated single-pass) will likely miss the same cross-file issues each time since the approach itself is the problem. Option D is a vague instruction that does not provide the structural mechanism needed for cross-file analysis."
