      "question": "A customer service AI agent handles multi-turn insurance claims conversations. After 15+ exchanges, the agent begins referring to the claimant's deductible as \"the previously discussed amount\" and the incident date as \"the date mentioned earlier,\" even though exact values ($2,500 and March 3rd) were stated in the second message. A junior engineer proposes adding a prompt reminder that says \"Remember to use exact numbers.\"\n\nWhat is the most effective fix for this behaviour?\n\n- **A.** Increase the model's context window size so it can retain more conversation history\n- **B.** Add a persistent case facts block containing structured key values (dates, amounts, policy numbers) that is included in every prompt and never summarised\n- **C.** Add a system prompt reminder instructing the model to always reference exact figures from earlier in the conversation\n- **D.** Switch to a model with better long-context recall benchmarks",
      "correctAnswer": "Add a persistent case facts block containing structured key values (dates, amounts, policy numbers) that is included in every prompt and never summarised.\n\nThis is the progressive summarisation trap, where the model condenses numerical values, dates, and percentages into vague summaries over extended conversations. The fix is a persistent case facts block \u2014 a structured block included in every prompt that is never summarised. Option A is incorrect because this is not a token limit issue; the information is present but gets summarised away. Option C is incorrect because prompt reminders do not structurally prevent summarisation. Option D is incorrect because the problem is architectural, not model-specific.",
      "explanation": "Add a persistent case facts block containing structured key values (dates, amounts, policy numbers) that is included in every prompt and never summarised.\n\nThis is the progressive summarisation trap, where the model condenses numerical values, dates, and percentages into vague summaries over extended conversations. The fix is a persistent case facts block \u2014 a structured block included in every prompt that is never summarised. Option A is incorrect because this is not a token limit issue; the information is present but gets summarised away. Option C is incorrect because prompt reminders do not structurally prevent summarisation. Option D is incorrect because the problem is architectural, not model-specific."
