      "question": "A multi-agent financial research system has three specialist agents: a market-data agent, a filings agent, and a news agent. The market-data agent encounters a rate-limit error when querying a stock API. Its error handler catches the exception and returns an empty JSON array `[]` to the orchestrator. The orchestrator interprets this as \"no market data available\" and proceeds to generate a research report noting \"no significant market activity\" for the period.\n\nWhich anti-pattern does this scenario illustrate, and why is it particularly harmful?\n\n- **A.** Workflow termination \u2014 the system should have killed the entire pipeline when the API failed instead of continuing with incomplete data\n- **B.** Silent suppression \u2014 returning an empty result as success is the worst anti-pattern because it prevents all downstream recovery by making the failure indistinguishable from a valid empty result\n- **C.** Upstream agent optimisation failure \u2014 the market-data agent returned too little data and should have included its full reasoning chain\n- **D.** Tool result trimming \u2014 the agent trimmed too aggressively and removed all market data fields from the response",
      "correctAnswer": "Silent suppression \u2014 returning an empty result as success is the worst anti-pattern because it prevents all downstream recovery by making the failure indistinguishable from a valid empty result.\n\nThis is the silent suppression anti-pattern, where an agent returns empty results as if they were successful outcomes. It is considered the worst error-handling anti-pattern because it prevents ALL recovery \u2014 no downstream agent or orchestrator can distinguish the failure from a legitimately empty result. Option A describes the workflow termination anti-pattern, which is also bad but at least visible; killing the pipeline is an overreaction but not silent. Option C misidentifies the problem; upstream agent optimisation is about returning structured data rather than verbose reasoning, which is unrelated. Option D is incorrect because tool result trimming is a deliberate context management technique, not an error condition.",
