      "question": "A large-scale code migration agent is tasked with converting a legacy Java monolith to microservices. After analysing 200+ classes across 30 packages, the agent begins its refactoring plan by writing: \"Based on typical patterns seen in enterprise Java applications, we should extract the service layer into microservices.\" However, its earlier exploration had discovered specific classes like `OrderProcessingFacade`, `InventoryBatchJob`, and `PaymentGatewayAdapter` with concrete dependency chains. An engineer suspects the model has hit its token limit.\n\nWhat is the most accurate diagnosis and the best mitigation?\n\n- **A.** The model has exceeded its token limit. Reduce the codebase scope to fit within the context window\n- **B.** This is context degradation \u2014 the model is losing grip on specific findings and falling back to generic patterns. The primary mitigation is using scratchpad files to write key findings to a file and read them back before the next phase\n- **C.** The model needs a stronger system prompt instructing it to always cite specific class names discovered during exploration\n- **D.** The agent should re-run the full codebase exploration at the start of each phase to refresh its memory of discovered classes",
