      "question": "An AI research assistant synthesises information from three sources about a company's quarterly revenue. Source A (company press release, January 15) states revenue of $4.2B. Source B (analyst report, February 20) states revenue of $4.1B. Source C (SEC filing, March 5) states revenue of $4.15B. The current implementation averages the three values and reports \"$4.15B in quarterly revenue.\"\n\nWhat is wrong with this approach, and what should the system do instead?\n\n- **A.** The system should select the SEC filing value as the most authoritative source and discard the other two\n- **B.** The system should annotate all three values with full attribution (source, date, document name) and present them together rather than selecting one or averaging\n- **C.** The system should report only the most recent value since newer sources supersede older ones\n- **D.** The system should flag this as a conflict and escalate to a human reviewer without presenting any value",
      "correctAnswer": "The system should annotate all three values with full attribution (source, date, document name) and present them together rather than selecting one or averaging.\n\nWhen sources conflict, the correct approach is to annotate BOTH (all) values with attribution \u2014 including source URL, document name, excerpt, and publication date \u2014 rather than selecting one or averaging. Additionally, temporal awareness matters: different dates may explain different numbers, representing a trend rather than a contradiction. Option A imposes a source hierarchy the system is not qualified to determine. Option C incorrectly assumes recency equals accuracy. Option D withholds useful information unnecessarily; the system should present annotated findings and let the consumer evaluate.",
