      "question": "A customer contacts a support AI agent about a billing discrepancy. After three exchanges, the customer types in all caps: \"THIS IS RIDICULOUS, I'VE BEEN CHARGED TWICE AND NO ONE IS HELPING ME.\" The agent's escalation logic detects high frustration sentiment and prepares to transfer to a human agent. However, the agent has not yet attempted to look up the customer's billing records or verify the duplicate charge.\n\nWhat is the correct handling of this situation?\n\n- **A.** Proceed with the escalation because high frustration sentiment is a reliable trigger, and the customer's emotional state takes priority over resolution attempts\n- **B.** Acknowledge the customer's frustration and attempt to resolve the issue, because frustration alone is not a reliable escalation trigger \u2014 a frustrated-but-resolvable situation should be acknowledged and resolved, reserving escalation for when the customer explicitly requests a human\n- **C.** Ignore the emotional language entirely and proceed with standard troubleshooting without acknowledgement, since sentiment should never influence agent behaviour\n- **D.** Ask the customer to rate their frustration on a scale-10 to determine whether the threshold for escalation has been met",
