      "question": "A team provides four few-shot examples showing Claude how to distinguish acceptable defensive null-check\npatterns from genuine error-handling bugs during code review. After deployment, the agent correctly identifies a\nproblematic error-handling pattern in a codebase written in a different language than any of the examples. What best\nexplains this behavior?\n\n* The model recognized the specific language syntax from its pre-training data and applied language-specific review.\n* **The few-shot examples taught the model the underlying judgment criteria for evaluating error handling, which it\n  generalized to the structurally similar but previously unseen pattern.**\n* The model defaulted to flagging the unfamiliar pattern because it did not match any known-acceptable examples.\n* The model decomposed the novel pattern into exact sub-patterns that matched elements from the few-shot examples.",
      "correctAnswer": "The few-shot examples taught the model the underlying judgment criteria for evaluating error handling, which it\ngeneralized to the structurally similar but previously unseen pattern.",
      "explanation": "This behavior is the hallmark of **semantic generalization** in Large Language Models. When provided with few-shot\nexamples, the model does not merely perform string or token matching; it learns the abstract logic\u2014the \"reasoning\nrubric\"\u2014that separates the examples into categories (e.g., intent, safety vs. negligence, and operational robustness).\nOnce the model internalizes the *logic* (what makes an error-handling bug \"bad\" vs. what makes a null-check \"\ndefensive\"), it can map that semantic understanding onto the syntax of any language it encountered during pre-training.\nIt essentially learns the *concept* of the bug rather than the *syntax* of the example."
