      "explanation": "LLMs often suffer from \"overconfidence bias,\" where their raw internal probability outputs (e.g., log-probs or\nself-reported confidence) do not map linearly to empirical accuracy. A raw confidence score.95 might actually\ncorrespond to an 80% accuracy rate, not a 95% one. Calibration is the process of using a labeled validation set to\nperform a statistical mapping between the model\u2019s *reported* confidence and the *observed* correctness. By applying\ntechniques such as Isotonic Regression or Platt Scaling, you create a calibration curve that translates the raw output\ninto a true probability. This allows you to set routing thresholds based on verifiable risk (e.g., \"route everything\nwith <98% calibrated probability\") rather than arbitrary raw scores."
