      "question": "A new engineer on the team reports that Claude Code is not following the project's API naming conventions\nduring code generation, even though other engineers on the same repository see the conventions applied correctly. What\nis the most effective first diagnostic step to identify the cause of this inconsistency?\n\n* Move all project configuration from `claude/rules/` into a single root-level `CLAUDE.md` to simplify the file\n  structure.\n* Reinstall Claude Code on the engineer's machine to clear any corrupted cached configurations.\n* **Ask the engineer to run the `/memory` command to verify which memory files are currently loaded in their session.**\n* Add the API naming conventions as inline comments in every source file so Claude Code reads them directly.",
      "correctAnswer": "Ask the engineer to run the `/memory` command to verify which memory files are currently loaded in their session.",
      "explanation": "The core of this issue lies in **Context Awareness**. An AI agent's performance is strictly bounded by the rules and\ndata it \"sees\" within its active context window. If the agent is not following naming conventions, it is highly likely\nthat the specific rule files (e.g., `api-conventions.md`) are not being indexed or loaded into the engineer's active\nsession.\n\nThe `/memory` command is the fundamental diagnostic tool for verifying the agent's internal state. It allows the\nengineer to inspect the *input context* before generation occurs. If the command output shows that the `.claude/rules/`\ndirectory is missing or that specific rule files are ignored, the cause is identified (e.g., local `.gitignore`\nexcluding the directory, corrupted local config, or the agent failing to recursively read the directory)."
