      "question": "Your agent handles high-ambiguity requests. A user states, \"My order was wrong, I want my money back.\" To achieve 80%\nFirst-Contact Resolution (FCR), the agent must act decisively but accurately. Which prompting strategy best balances\nautonomy with error reduction?\n\n* Configure the system prompt to immediately trigger `process_refund` whenever a customer mentions \"money back\" to\n  prioritize speed.\n* Use a \"Chain-of-Thought\" (CoT) system instruction that mandates the agent to call `lookup_order` and `get_customer`\n  first, then evaluate the policy before deciding to call `process_refund`.\n* Instruct the agent to present all policy documents to the customer before asking for confirmation to process any\n  refund.\n* Limit the agent's tools to `escalate_to_human` only, forcing a human to handle all refund requests to ensure 0% error\n  rate.",
      "correctAnswer": "Use a \"Chain-of-Thought\" (CoT) system instruction that mandates the agent to call `lookup_order` and `get_customer`\nfirst, then evaluate the policy before deciding to call `process_refund`.",
      "explanation": "High-ambiguity requests require the agent to build a **semantic state** before taking destructive actions.\n\n* **Sequential Dependency:** By forcing the agent to fetch context (`lookup_order`, `get_customer`) *before* acting, you\n  ensure the model has the necessary data to perform an \"if-this-then-that\" evaluation against your business logic.\n* **Reasoning Budget:** Chain-of-Thought forces the model to articulate its reasoning (\"The order was delivered 3 days\n  ago, and our policy is 30 days, so this is valid\"). This explicit reasoning significantly reduces hallucinations and\n  prevents premature execution of tools."
