      "question": "Production logs show the agent sometimes selects `get_customer` when `lookup_order` would be more appropriate for\nambiguous requests like \"I need help with my recent purchase.\" You decide to add few-shot examples to your system\nprompt. Which approach will most effectively address this issue?\n\n* A) Add examples grouped by tool\u2014all `get_customer` scenarios together, then all `lookup_order` scenarios.\n* B) Add explicit \"use when\" and \"do not use when\" guidelines in each tool's description.\n* **C) Add 4-6 examples targeting ambiguous scenarios, each showing reasoning for why one tool was chosen over plausible\n  alternatives.**\n* D) Add 10-15 examples of clear, unambiguous requests that demonstrate correct tool selection for typical use cases.",
      "correctAnswer": "Add 4-6 examples targeting ambiguous scenarios, each showing reasoning for why one tool was chosen over plausible\nalternatives.",
      "explanation": "The core of this problem is **Decision Boundary Ambiguity**. The agent is struggling to distinguish between similar\nintent categories when the user input lacks specific keywords (like \"order ID\").\n\n* **Contrastive Reasoning:** By providing examples that explicitly include \"Reasoning\" or \"Chain-of-Thought\" (CoT), you\n  guide the model through the mental process of rejecting a tool. Showing the model *why* `get_customer` is the wrong\n  choice for \"recent purchase\" (e.g., \"The user mentioned 'purchase', which implies a transaction, not an account\n  profile update\") creates a stronger, more transferable logical rule than simply showing a list of correct\n  associations.\n* **Focus on Edge Cases:** Few-shot examples are most effective when they target the \"confusion zone.\" Providing\n  examples of clear, typical use cases (Option D) does little to correct the agent's behavior on the specific ambiguous\n  edge cases where it is currently failing."
