      "question": "Your agent uses progressive summarization for context management. Logs show that critical details (like specific\ndiscount amounts) are lost when older turns are summarized into vague generalities. What is the most effective fix to\nensure transactional facts are retained?\n\n* A) Store full conversation history externally and implement retrieval to search it when reference phrases occur.\n* **B) Extract transactional facts (amounts, dates, order numbers) into a persistent \"case facts\" block included in each\n  prompt, outside the summarized history.**\n* C) Revise the summarization prompt to explicitly preserve all numerical values, percentages, dates, and\n  customer-stated expectations verbatim.\n* D) Increase the summarization threshold from 70% to 85% capacity.",
      "correctAnswer": "Extract transactional facts (amounts, dates, order numbers) into a persistent \"case facts\" block included in each\nprompt, outside the summarized history.",
      "explanation": "This solution relies on the architectural principle of **State Decoupling**. You must separate *Transactional State* (\nthe \"Truth\") from *Conversational History* (the \"Context\").\n\n* **State Persistence:** By maintaining a dedicated `case_facts` block, you create an \"immutable source of truth\" that\n  the agent has access to at every single turn. This block is not subject to the destructive compression of\n  summarization algorithms. The agent is no longer forced to \"recall\" a value from a 20-turn-old summary; it simply\n  reads it from the provided metadata.\n* **Reduction of Cognitive Load:** This approach reduces the model's reliance on its attention mechanism to \"search\"\n  through long, compressed summaries. It provides the model with the necessary parameters immediately, leading to higher\n  accuracy in transactional tasks."
