      "question": "**Scenario: Monorepo Rule Scoping**\nA monorepo has Python pipelines, TypeScript APIs, and protobufs. The team wants type-checking reminders only when\nediting Python files. What is the most token-efficient approach?\n\n* A) Add all rules to project .claude/CLAUDE.md with a comment about Python only.\n* B) Create CLAUDE.md inside pipelines/ directory.\n* **C) Create .claude/rules/ file with paths: ['pipelines/**/*.py'] so rules load only for matching files.**\n* D) Use @import to always include typing-rules.md in project CLAUDE.md.",
      "correctAnswer": "Create .claude/rules/ file with paths: ['pipelines/**/*.py'] so rules load only for matching files.",
      "explanation": "This approach leverages **context-aware rule injection**. Claude Code and modern agentic architectures operate by\ninjecting instructions into the system prompt. Injecting the entire rule set into the global system prompt (Options A\nand D) wastes tokens on every single turn, regardless of whether the user is editing a `.ts` file or a `.proto` file.\n\nBy utilizing path-based scoping in `.claude/rules/`:\n\n1. **Context Window Optimization:** The agent only evaluates the Python type-checking rules when the file context\n   matches the provided glob pattern.\n2. **Reduced Interference:** It prevents \"prompt leakage\" where the agent might mistakenly apply Python-specific linting\n   conventions to TypeScript files.\n3. **Efficiency:** Only the relevant instructions are loaded into the prompt's `System` or `Instruction` block,\n   significantly reducing the token overhead for non-Python file edits."
