      "question": "Your automated code reviews identify valid issues but lack actionability (e.g., vague \"potential null pointer\"\nwarnings). Even with explicit instructions, the model remains inconsistent. Which **prompting technique** most reliably\nensures consistently actionable feedback?\n\n* **A) Add 3-4 few-shot examples showing the exact format you want: issue identified, code location, specific fix\n  suggestion.**\n* B) Further refine the instructions with more explicit requirements for each part of the feedback format.\n* C) Implement a two-pass approach where one prompt identifies issues and a second prompt generates fixes.\n* D) Expand the context window to include more of the surrounding codebase.",
      "correctAnswer": "Add 3-4 few-shot examples showing the exact format you want: issue identified, code location, specific fix\nsuggestion.",
      "explanation": "This relies on **Few-Shot Prompting**, which is statistically superior to Zero-Shot (instruction-only) prompting for\nforcing structural consistency in LLM outputs.\n\n* **Pattern Matching vs. Interpretation:** When you provide instructions (\"be detailed\"), the model interprets these\n  qualitatively. When you provide examples, the model performs *pattern completion*. It doesn't need to \"understand\"\n  what you mean by actionable; it simply replicates the structure and depth of the provided samples.\n* **Output Predictability:** By providing concrete examples of \"Code Input -> Feedback Output,\" you define the schema of\n  the expected response. The model will adhere to the style, verbosity, and specific fix-suggestion format established\n  in your examples, significantly reducing variance across different PRs."
