      "question": "Your automated code review averages 15 findings per PR with a 40% false positive rate. Developers are spending too much\ntime investigating each finding to determine validity. Stakeholders have strictly rejected any filtering or hiding of\nfindings. What change would best address the investigation time bottleneck?\n\n* Configure Claude to only surface findings it assesses as high confidence, filtering out uncertain flags before\n  developers see them.\n* Add a post-processor that analyzes finding patterns and automatically suppresses those matching historical false\n  positive signatures.\n* Categorize findings as \"blocking issues\" versus \"suggestions\" with tiered review requirements.\n* **Require Claude to include its reasoning and confidence assessment inline with each finding.",
      "correctAnswer": "Require Claude to include its reasoning and confidence assessment inline with each finding.",
      "explanation": "When you cannot filter findings, you must **optimize the triage process**. The current bottleneck is \"\nclick-to-investigate\" latency. By forcing the model to display its reasoning and confidence score *inline* (directly in\nthe comment or dashboard view), you enable \"at-a-glance\" triage.\n\n* **Cognitive Load Reduction:** A developer can look at a finding: \"Potential null pointer (Confidence: 0.4,\n  Reasoning: 'Variable X might be null because it is not checked before usage').\" The developer instantly recognizes\n  this is a low-confidence false positive and dismisses it without opening the file or deeper investigation.\n* **Triage Efficiency:** This transforms the workflow from *investigative* (clicking to see why) to *evaluative* (\n  reading the provided logic). Providing the reasoning directly removes the need for context-switching, which is the\n  primary driver of the investigation bottleneck."
