      "question": "Your team uses a `/commit` skill defined in the project repository. One developer wants to customize this for their\npersonal workflow without affecting teammates. What should you recommend?\n\n* A) Add username-based conditional logic to the project skill's frontmatter.\n* B) Create a personal version at `~/.claude/skills/commit/SKILL.md` with the same name.\n* **C) Create a personal version in `~/.claude/skills/` with a different name like `/my-commit`.**\n* D) Set `override: true` in the personal skill's frontmatter to take precedence over the project version.",
      "correctAnswer": "Create a personal version in `~/.claude/skills/` with a different name like `/my-commit`.",
      "explanation": "This approach maintains the **Integrity of Shared Tooling** and prevents **Namespace Collisions**.\n\n* **Namespace Safety:** By using a unique name (e.g., `/my-commit`), you ensure that the developer does not accidentally\n  invoke the team-standard skill, nor do they accidentally overwrite it. It provides a clean separation between \"\n  Global/Team Standards\" and \"Local Customization.\"\n* **Avoiding Complexity:** Creating a separate command avoids the \"works on my machine\" syndrome. If the developer later\n  wants to share their improvements, they can simply move the logic back into the project repository without having to\n  untangle conditional logic or overrides.\n* **Clarity:** It is explicit. When the developer types the command, they know exactly which version is executing."
