      "correctAnswer": "To run commands before or after Claude executes a tool.",
      "explanation": "Hooks in Claude Code serve as **Lifecycle Interception points**. They allow developers to inject custom logic into the\nagent's decision-making and execution workflow at specific temporal markers relative to tool execution.\n\n* **Interception Logic:** By hooking into the agent's loop, you can inspect inputs (arguments), modify them, or block\n  them entirely before they hit the system runtime (Pre-hooks), or log outputs, perform post-processing, and analyze\n  usage telemetry after the system has completed the action (Post-hooks).\n\n\n* **Control and Security:** This mechanism is the architectural foundation for agent governance. Without hooks, an agent\n  is a \"black box\" that executes whatever it outputs. Hooks enable the \"Human-in-the-Loop\" (HITL) patterns, security\n  guardrails (like the PreToolUse hook for blocking sensitive files), and automated validation routines that are\n  necessary to make agentic coding reliable in enterprise environments."
