      "question": "Claude Code generates suggestions, but you notice a pattern: subtle issues like performance regressions or broken edge cases only surface when a different team member reviews the PR. Claude's own reasoning during generation shows it considered these cases but concluded they were correct.\n\n**Which approach directly addresses the root cause of this self-review limitation?**\n\n- **A.** Enable extended thinking mode\n- **B.** Add explicit self-review instructions to the generation prompt\n- **C.** Have a second, independent Claude instance review the changes without seeing the generator's reasoning\n- **D.** Include comprehensive test files in the prompt",
      "correctAnswer": "Have a second, independent Claude instance review the changes.\n\nUtilizing an independent instance for review eliminates \"Confirmation Bias.\" If the same model instance reviews its own work, it is likely to persist in the same logical errors. A separate instance provides the necessary \"fresh eyes\" required for effective verification.",
      "explanation": "Have a second, independent Claude instance review the changes.\n\nUtilizing an independent instance for review eliminates \"Confirmation Bias.\" If the same model instance reviews its own work, it is likely to persist in the same logical errors. A separate instance provides the necessary \"fresh eyes\" required for effective verification."
