      "question": "The team's initial code review prompt sends the entire PR diff (sometimes 500+ lines across 15+ files) in a single Claude Code invocation. Reviews are inconsistent \u2014 catching issues in some files but missing obvious problems in others.\n\n**What is causing this, and how should the team restructure the review?**\n\n- **A.** The model's `max_tokens` is too low \u2014 increasing it will improve review thoroughness\n- **B.** This is attention dilution \u2014 when too much content is sent in a single prompt, the model's attention is spread thin and it misses details. The team should implement a multi-pass architecture: review each file or logical group separately, then combine findings\n- **C.** The model is not powerful enough for code review \u2014 switch to a larger model\n- **D.** Add more explicit instructions to the prompt telling Claude to \"review every file carefully\"",
      "correctAnswer": "This is attention dilution.\n\nThis is attention dilution \u2014 when too much content (500+ lines across 15+ files) is presented in a single prompt, the model's attention is distributed across all the content, causing it to miss details in individual files. The fix is a multi-pass architecture: review each file or logical group in a separate Claude Code invocation, then optionally combine findings. This ensures focused attention on each piece of code. Increasing max_tokens (A) does not solve attention distribution. Adding \"be thorough\" instructions (D) does not overcome the fundamental attention limitation.",
      "explanation": "This is attention dilution.\n\nThis is attention dilution \u2014 when too much content (500+ lines across 15+ files) is presented in a single prompt, the model's attention is distributed across all the content, causing it to miss details in individual files. The fix is a multi-pass architecture: review each file or logical group in a separate Claude Code invocation, then optionally combine findings. This ensures focused attention on each piece of code. Increasing max_tokens (A) does not solve attention distribution. Adding \"be thorough\" instructions (D) does not overcome the fundamental attention limitation."
