      "question": "Some documents have fields that may legitimately not be present (e.g., a \"discount\" field on an invoice that has no discount). The team notices that when a field is marked as required in the schema, Claude sometimes fabricates a value rather than leaving it blank.\n\n**How should the schema handle optional or missing data?**\n\n- **A.** Mark all fields as required and add a prompt instruction to \"leave fields empty if not found\"\n- **B.** Define fields that may be absent as nullable or optional in the tool's parameter schema, explicitly allowing `null` values \u2014 this gives Claude a valid way to represent \"not found\" without fabricating data\n- **C.** Use a sentinel value like `\"N/A\"` for missing fields and require Claude to output that string\n- **D.** Remove optional fields from the schema entirely so Claude never encounters them",
      "correctAnswer": "Define fields as nullable or optional in the parameter schema.\n\nDefining fields as nullable or optional in the parameter schema gives Claude a legitimate way to represent \"data not found\" by returning `null`. Without this, when a field is required, Claude may fabricate a plausible value rather than violating the schema. Prompt instructions to \"leave fields empty\" (A) conflict with a required schema constraint. Sentinel values (C) mix metadata with data. Removing fields (D) means you can never extract them even when they are present.",
