      "question": "The team has implemented a self-review step: after Claude extracts data, the same session sends the extraction back to Claude and asks it to verify the results. The team notices that the self-review almost never catches errors that were in the original extraction.\n\n**Why is this happening, and how should they address it?**\n\n- **A.** The self-review model is not powerful enough \u2014 use a larger model for verification\n- **B.** Self-review within the same session retains the original reasoning context, making it likely to repeat the same errors. The team should use a separate session or a different verification approach (e.g., programmatic validation rules, a separate Claude session with only the document and extracted data, or human spot-checks) to get an independent review\n- **C.** The self-review prompt is not detailed enough \u2014 adding more specific review instructions will fix the issue\n- **D.** Self-review works well in general but fails on extraction tasks specifically due to the structured output format",
      "correctAnswer": "Self-review within the same session retains original reasoning context.\n\nSelf-review within the same session is fundamentally limited because the session retains the original reasoning context. Claude is essentially reviewing its own work with the same \"state of mind\" that produced it, making it likely to repeat the same errors and overlook the same issues. Effective verification requires an independent perspective: a separate Claude session that receives only the document and the extraction (not the original reasoning), programmatic validation rules, or human review. Prompt quality (C) does not overcome the fundamental same-session bias.",
