      "question": "**Scenario: Jira Integration Strategy**\nYour team needs to integrate Claude Code with Jira. A developer proposes building a custom MCP server from scratch for \"\nfull control.\" What is the recommended approach?\n\n* A) Build a custom MCP server to ensure exact control over endpoints.\n* **B) Use the existing community MCP server for Jira; reserve custom builds for genuinely unique workflows.**\n* C) Connect directly to the Jira REST API via a generic tool.\n* D) Embed Jira API credentials in the system prompt.",
      "correctAnswer": "Use the existing community MCP server for Jira; reserve custom builds for genuinely unique workflows.",
      "explanation": "The \"Build vs. Buy\" analysis for MCP servers is straightforward: **standardize on community-maintained integrations for\ncommon SaaS APIs.\n\n* **Maintenance Debt:** Jira's API surface is vast and frequently updated. A custom server requires you to mirror those\n  updates, handle API versioning, and manage authentication refreshes manually. Community servers offload this\n  maintenance burden to the ecosystem, ensuring your agent remains compatible without dedicated engineering time.\n* **Standardization:** Community servers implement standardized schemas for common tasks (creating issues, updating\n  statuses, searching tickets). This consistency allows the agent to reason about Jira using a predictable API structure\n  that it has likely already seen during training or in prior usage."
