      "question": "A financial analysis system extracts quarterly earnings data using a 'DataMiner' subagent and generates a chart using\na 'Grapher' subagent. When the DataMiner passes its findings back to the coordinator as a conversational summary, and\nthe coordinator passes that summary to the Grapher, the Grapher frequently plots incorrect numbers or mislabels axes.\nWhat is the most robust architectural solution to this context-passing issue?\n\n* **A) Instruct the DataMiner to output its findings as a structured CSV or JSON array and have the coordinator pass the\n  raw data directly to the Grapher.**\n* B) Add a \"Reviewer\" agent to check the Grapher's work against the DataMiner's summary before finalizing the output.\n* C) Increase the coordinator's temperature to allow it to more creatively summarize the financial data for the Grapher.\n* D) Instruct the Grapher to call the DataMiner directly to verify the numbers before plotting.",
      "correctAnswer": "Instruct the DataMiner to output its findings as a structured CSV or JSON array and have the coordinator pass the raw\ndata directly to the Grapher.",
      "explanation": "The failure here is **Semantic Degradation** caused by conversational \"telephoning.\"\n\n* **Structured vs. Unstructured:** When financial data (which requires 100% precision) is converted into a \"\n  conversational summary,\" the LLM coordinator may round numbers, omit decimal places, or lose the relationship between\n  headers and values.\n* **Data Integrity:** Passing data in a structured format (JSON/CSV) ensures the Grapher receives the exact figures\n  without the \"noise\" of natural language interpretation. LLMs are much more reliable at parsing JSON into tool\n  parameters (for graphing) than they are at extracting raw numbers from a chatty summary."
