      "question": "During a materials research task, the web search subagent queries three source categories with different\noutcomes: academic databases returned 15 relevant papers, industry reports returned \"0 results found,\" and patent\ndatabases returned \"Connection timeout.\" When designing error propagation to the coordinator, what approach enables the\nbest recovery decisions?\n\n* **Distinguish access failures (timeout) needing retry decisions from valid empty results (\"0 results\") representing\n  successful queries.**\n* Have the subagent retry transient failures internally and only report persistent errors.\n* Aggregate outcomes into a single success rate metric (e.g., \"67% source coverage\") with detailed logs available on\n  request.\n* Report both the timeout and \"0 results\" as failures requiring coordinator intervention.",
      "correctAnswer": "Distinguish access failures (timeout) needing retry decisions from valid empty results (\"0 results\") representing\nsuccessful queries.",
      "explanation": "The coordinator can only make correct recovery decisions if it receives semantically accurate status information per\nsource. A timeout and an empty result are categorically different: a timeout indicates an infrastructure or connectivity\nfailure where the query did not execute \u2014 retry is a valid recovery action. An empty result indicates the query\nexecuted successfully and the source contains no matching records \u2014 retry is pointless and alternative sourcing or\nscope adjustment is the appropriate response. Collapsing these into the same error category or an aggregate metric\ndestroys the information the coordinator needs to select the correct recovery path for each outcome."
