      "question": "You observe significant overlap between the Web Search Agent and the Document Analysis Agent, causing token usage to\ndouble without increasing research depth. What is the most effective architectural approach to eliminate this\nredundancy?\n\n* Allow both agents to complete their parallel work, then have the coordinator deduplicate overlapping findings before\n  passing to the synthesis agent.\n* Implement a shared state mechanism where agents log their current focus area, allowing other agents to dynamically\n  avoid duplicating work in progress.\n* **Have the coordinator explicitly partition the research space before delegation, assigning distinct subtopics or\n  source types to each agent.**\n* Convert to sequential execution where document analysis runs only after web search completes, using the web search\n  findings as context to avoid duplication.",
      "correctAnswer": "Have the coordinator explicitly partition the research space before delegation, assigning distinct subtopics or source\ntypes to each agent.",
      "explanation": "This solution leverages **Deterministic Orchestration**, the most effective way to manage agent behavior in a production\nMulti-Agent System (MAS).\n\n* **Divide and Conquer:** The Coordinator is the only entity with the \"global\" view of the research goal. By performing\n  task decomposition *before* delegation, the Coordinator transforms a broad, ambiguous prompt into discrete,\n  non-overlapping sub-tasks (e.g., Agent A focuses on \"Market Trends via Web Search,\" Agent B focuses on \"Internal\n  Historical Data via Document Analysis\").\n* **Efficiency:** This approach eliminates redundancy at the source. By enforcing boundaries upfront, you ensure that\n  agents utilize their specific toolsets (Search vs. Local Docs) on distinct domains, maximizing the value of every\n  token spent."
